  <h1>Mabi</h1>
  
 </div>
 <div class="page-nav"><span class="page-info">Page 3 of 3</span><div class="page-links"><a href="/mabi?page=2#content" class="page-prev">&#8592; Previous</a><span class="page-next disabled">Next &#8594;</span></div></div>
 <div class="story-body" id="content">
  <p>Mabi said: You can carry the name or you can set it down. You can be the one who sees the water the way your grandmother saw it, and her grandmother before her — or you can be a woman who lives on an island and fishes and grows old and is remembered by the people who knew her but is not remembered by the water. Both lives are real. Both lives are complete. I am offering you both.</p>
<p>Mabi stood in the water and felt the weight of what was being offered. She was seventeen. She did not know what she wanted, did not know if she had ever known what she wanted, did not know if wanting was something she was capable of or something the world allowed people to do before it told them what they would become.</p>
<p>She thought about her mother, who had left the island and built a life elsewhere, who had chosen the mainland and the ordinary and the safe. She thought about the way her mother's face changed when she talked about the island — the tightness around the eyes, the jaw set against something that was not quite grief and not quite anger but lived in the space between.</p>
<p>She thought about her grandmother, who had never left, who had carried the name and the water and the sight and the terrible weight of knowing things about the island that no one else could see, who had died with an expression on her face that Mabi now understood, that was not peace but was recognition, the expression of someone who had finally been allowed to put something down.</p>
<p>How do I set it down? Mabi asked.</p>
<p>You don't, Mabi said. You can't. Once you've called me, once you've seen me, once you've stood in the water and felt the stillness — you can't unknow it. You can't go back to before. But you can choose not to carry it forward. You can choose to see the water the way everyone else sees it — as water, as landscape, as the backdrop to the life you're living. And I will still be here. I will still know what I know. But I will not bother you with it. You can live a life that is entirely yours.</p>
<p>And if I carry it?</p>
<p>Then you carry it, Mabi said. And you see what I see. And you know what I know. And you become the one who stands between the island and the water, the one who speaks for both, the one who holds the shape of the safe places and the shape of the unsafe places and the shape of the places where the water turns. It is a heavy thing. Your grandmother carried it for sixty years. It wore her down to the bone. But it is also — Mabi paused, and the water around her shifted, became something warmer, something that was almost physical, almost present, almost the hand of an old woman on her shoulder. It is also a gift. Even the heavy things are gifts, if you choose them freely.</p>
<p>Mabi stood in the water. The moon was moving toward the horizon. The tide was beginning to turn. She could feel the moment approaching — the moment when Mabi would stop waiting and the water would resume its current and the choice would solidify into something that could not be unmade.</p>
<p>She thought about the island. The house with the blue shutters. Old Pauley and his boat. The fish in the reef and the water in the wells and the rain on the rocks. She thought about her mother, who had not chosen, who had simply run, who had left and never come back except for the funeral and three days after.</p>
<p>She thought about her grandmother's face at the end.</p>
<p>She opened her mouth to speak, and then she closed it, because she felt — she felt in the water, she felt in the pressure and the temperature and the movement of the current — that the choice was not words. The choice was what she did next. The choice was whether she stepped forward into the water or stepped back onto the beach.</p>
<p>She stepped forward. She walked deeper until the water was at her chest, at her shoulders, at her chin. She did not go deeper. She did not need to. The choice had been made in the stepping, not in the depth.</p>
<p>Mabi, she said. One more time. Not calling this time. Acknowledging.</p>
<p>And Mabi was there — Mabi was always there — and Mabi flowed around her and through her and into her, and the water went dark for a moment, for a long moment, for a moment that was longer than time, and when it came back to light Mabi was sitting on the beach with the sand cold beneath her and the tide coming in and the sky in the east already beginning to blush with the first suggestion of dawn.</p>
<p>She stood. She walked up the beach. She picked up her shoes. She walked back to the house with the blue shutters, and she sat on the porch, and she watched the sun come up over the island, over the water, over the place where the reef began and the water changed color and the old shape of things showed itself to anyone who knew how to look.</p>
<p>She knew how to look now. She had always known. But now she knew that she knew, and the water was showing her everything — the currents and the pressures and the deep rock formations and the turns and the returns and the places where the island held the water and the water held the island and both of them together held the name that had been there since before memory and would be there until after the island itself was gone.</p>
<p>Mabi. She said it to herself, to the house, to the morning. The syllables still felt like swallowing sea glass. But they were hers now. They belonged to her in a way that nothing had ever belonged to her before.</p>
<p>She sat on the porch and watched the island wake up — the fishermen going to the boats, the goats starting to move in the inland pastures, the smoke rising from the kitchen of the one neighbor who was always up early. She watched the island do the things it did every morning, the ordinary things, the things that people who did not know how to look would see and understand as simply an island being an island.</p>
<p>But she saw more than that. She saw the water moving through the island the way blood moves through a body. She saw the shape of the safe places and the unsafe places, the old map overlaid on the new chart, the names that corresponded to no names on any official record. She saw Mabi everywhere — not as a figure, not as a face, but as a quality of attention that lived in the water and the rock and the soil and the roots of the plants that grew in the thin, sandy soil.</p>
<p>She saw it, and she held it, and she carried it, and the weight was exactly what her grandmother had promised — heavy, and a gift.</p>
<p>She went inside. She made coffee. She took down the old map from the drawer where her grandmother had kept it and she spread it on the kitchen table and she looked at the shapes of the places that no longer existed and she began, slowly, carefully, to learn them by heart.</p>
<p>There was work to do. The safe places were shifting. The water was changing. The island was becoming something that the old charts and the new charts both failed to account for, and someone needed to watch it, someone needed to speak for it, someone needed to carry the name that lived in the rocks and the wells and the deep places and the water that remembered everything the ocean forgot.</p>
<p>She was seventeen. She was the one.</p>
<p>And Mabi, in the water, in the rock, in the deep places beneath the island — Mabi was pleased.</p>
<p><em>End of story.</em></p>
